 "The library/writing-coherence section polices AI-assisted and pass-written prose at the joints where local fluency hides global incoherence: each of its 23 patterns names one axis (claim exposure vs citation density, restatement vs development, announcement vs performance, formal compression vs instance grounding, warmth vs proposition, coverage vs specificity) and forces the writer to pick an explicit composition rather than leave the mismatch implicit. Collectively the section detects the signature failures of drafting in passes — bolded claims on thin citations, drifted claim wordings across sections, undefined terms at first mention, fused multi-claim paragraphs, missing section bridges, stale references after restructure, triads and balanced pairs doing padding work, hedged lifts and undischarged promises — and it composes as a checklist applied by audit (per-section pattern audits, typed diagnostic annotations, and verified rewrites drawn from each pattern’s COMPOSITIONS menu), with the more structural members (meta-title, meta-lede, plain-language-thesis, one-paragraph-per-typed-claim) governing what the reader meets at high-visibility positions and the fail-closed members (citation-density, name-what-you-drop, stale-reference) governing what the argument owes at its load-bearing ones, all under the shared restraint that patterns follow from documented use-cases rather than speculation.",
